Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: A Hawser Mends the Fog
- Story & Context: When an unflagged Liberian freighter scraped the quay at Pier 42 in Rotterdam, the impact severed an auxiliary shore cable and cast the entire terminal into pulsing chartreuse emergency light. Bram stood atop a stack of shipping containers in the biting salt spray, smelling diesel exhaust and cold brine, as two lost cargo vessels out in the channel traded eight-second warning bellows across the zero-visibility water. He slammed the master fader on his sampler to match their blasts with tectonic 808 sub-swells, screaming into a megaphone: "Sew the iron shut before the morning drowns us." The harbor police mistook the synchronized low-end reverberations for a structural gantry collapse, clearing two terminals while fifty ravers continued dancing blind in the damp diesel haze.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: High-contrast film still of a massive frayed mooring hawser cable slicing through dense oceanic fog over oily harbor water, illuminated by a sickly green emergency light, with the ghostly outline of a cargo ship hull looming in the background, album art typography subtle and weathered.

Track 2: Did You Cut the Warp Line?
- Story & Context: In the cramped wheelhouse of the derelict tugboat Albatross, Annika blew on her raw, frozen knuckles while rewiring an overdriven Korg MS-2000 synth pad directly into an illegal shortwave antenna. She was hiding from her older brother, a licensed estuary pilot who had threatened to call the port authority after recognizing her baritone pad drones interfering with harbor traffic signals. The cabin reeked of spilled chicory coffee, wet wool, and hot vacuum tubes, trembling violently every time a sea-bound tanker answered her low D-flat frequency with an automated blast. Overcome by the terrible isolation of the straits, she leaned into a dripping ribbon microphone and confessed: "I answered every ship that never called my name."
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Atmospheric shot inside a rain-streaked, fogged tugboat bridge at night, glowing vacuum tubes and vintage rack synthesizers tangled in wet wiring on an old navigational chart table, cold maritime blue darkness outside the window.

Track 3: Katendrecht Null-Point
- Story & Context: Long after the city council shuttered the dry-docks and installed acoustic jamming arrays to halt the unlicensed estuary raves, older dockers at Katendrecht began reporting ghost acoustics drifting over the low tide. Watchmen reported smelling ozone and rotting kelp on windless November nights, watching mysterious ripples fan across the basin while phantom big-beat drum breaks shook the concrete pilings without any sound system in sight. Pilots approaching the Maas still experience unexplainable sonar phasing at the fairway entrance, as if the riverbed itself has permanently memorized the call-and-response between the ships and Vos’s fuzz-drenched bass. Scratched with a welding torch onto the harbor wall where the scene finally dissolved lies their definitive epitaph: "We threw the thread into the dark and felt the ocean pull."
